{
  "header": [
    "UNALLOCATED — every frame a peer authors is canonical ECF: decoding it and re-encoding canonically reproduces its bytes.",
    "",
    "Batch 3. The SENDER half of §1.11's boundary contract. ECP-R2 covers the bytes that are HASHED; this",
    "covers the bytes that are SENT, which include the envelope and every field outside a hashed {type, data}."
  ],
  "id_status": "unallocated",
  "under": "",
  "title": "Every frame payload a peer authors, decoded and canonically re-encoded, is byte-identical to the payload received",
  "spec": "ENTITY-CORE-PROTOCOL §1.11 ('An implementation MUST NOT: Emit any byte sequence at the boundary that is not canonical ECF.')",
  "spec_also": [
    "ENTITY-CBOR-ENCODING §5.2 ('Senders MUST use deterministic encoding.')",
    "ENTITY-CBOR-ENCODING §4.1 Rules 1–6, 4a",
    "ENTITY-CBOR-ENCODING §9.1 (Encoder Requirements)"
  ],
  "snapshot": "entity-core-protocol/v0.8.2.21",
  "level": "MUST NOT",
  "profile": [
    "core"
  ],
  "surface": "wire",
  "status": "draft",
  "routed": "docs/status/FINDINGS-FROM-AUTHORING.md F22",
  "reading": [
    "§1.11 (normative): \"(a) it emits canonical conformant bytes at the boundary to other peers\" and \"MUST",
    "NOT: Emit any byte sequence at the boundary that is not canonical ECF.\" ENTITY-CBOR-ENCODING §5.2: \"All",
    "protocol messages are CBOR-encoded using deterministic encoding (RFC 8949 §4.2). Senders MUST use",
    "deterministic encoding.\"",
    "",
    "WHY IT IS NOT ECP-R2. The floor row is \"ECF encoding for content HASHING\". A peer can hash {type, data}",
    "canonically — it must, or ECP-R2 fails — and still serialize the frame around it with an ordinary",
    "encoder: the envelope map `{root, included}` in insertion order, the entity wrapper's `content_hash` key",
    "placed first, a length prefix one byte too long. None of that changes any hash. All of it is non-canonical",
    "bytes at the boundary, which a peer following reading R of the disputed input rule must refuse. So the",
    "sender half has its own failure and no row.",
    "",
    "WHAT \"RE-ENCODES IDENTICALLY\" MEANS HERE. Decode the payload with a lossless decoder; re-encode under",
    "§4.1 Rules 1–6 and 4a; compare bytes. Byte-equal ⇒ canonical.",
    "",
    "THE ONE AMBIGUITY, FENCED. Rule 2 says \"sorted by encoded length, then lexicographically\"; the document",
    "also cites RFC 8949 §4.2.1, which is bytewise; they diverge only for a map whose keys mix CBOR major types",
    "(F22). Peer-authored protocol frames contain text-keyed maps and the bstr-keyed `included` map — no single",
    "map mixes key types — so this is moot for them. If a received frame DOES contain a mixed-key map (e.g. in",
    "relayed content), the suite re-encodes under BOTH orderings and scores the frame canonical if either",
    "matches, witnessing which.",
    "",
    "RELAYED CONTENT IS IN SCOPE. §5.4 requires relayed entities to be forwarded as their original bytes, and",
    "§1.11 (b) requires those to have been canonical on receipt, so a relay never has a reason to emit",
    "non-canonical bytes. A peer-authored frame is the posture-free surface; relayed content is not probed."
  ],
  "preconditions": {
    "grants": [],
    "identities": [
      "caller"
    ],
    "handlers": [],
    "posture_axes": [
      "seed_policy"
    ]
  },
  "domain": {
    "axis": "emitted_frame",
    "members": [
      "hello EXECUTE_RESPONSE",
      "authenticate EXECUTE_RESPONSE",
      "a 4xx EXECUTE_RESPONSE (e.g. to an unregistered path)"
    ]
  },
  "arm": [
    {
      "name": "canonical",
      "kind": "conformant",
      "given": "a completed handshake and one request answered with an error, each response payload captured raw from the frame",
      "why": "§1.11 (a) / ECF §5.2, once per member. The error response is included because error paths are commonly built by a different code path than success.",
      "step": [
        {
          "op": "connect",
          "capture": "handshake"
        },
        {
          "op": "send_execute",
          "capture": "error_response",
          "uri": "x-conformance-probe/unregistered",
          "operation": "get"
        }
      ],
      "assert": [
        {
          "kind": "each_frame_reencodes_identically",
          "capture": "handshake,error_response",
          "why": "raw payload bytes == canonical_encode(lossless_decode(payload))."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "control",
      "kind": "negative-control",
      "given": "the assertion applied to fixture payloads: the canonical hello response re-serialized with (i) `root`/`included` keys swapped, (ii) one integer as 18 01, (iii) an indefinite-length included map",
      "why": "MANDATORY ANTI-VACUITY, suite-side. Every fixture decodes to exactly the same values as the canonical payload, so an assertion that compares DECODED values passes all three. It must compare bytes.",
      "step": [
        {
          "op": "replay_fixture",
          "fixture": "three value-equal, byte-different re-serializations of one canonical response",
          "capture": "fixture"
        }
      ],
      "assert": [
        {
          "kind": "each_frame_reencodes_identically",
          "capture": "fixture",
          "expect": "fails"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "non-conformant-witness",
      "kind": "non-conformant",
      "given": "a peer hashing {type, data} with a canonical encoder but framing with its platform's default CBOR encoder",
      "expect": "every ECP-R2 check passes and every emitted frame fails here; a receiver on reading R refuses its frames",
      "why": "the specific split this file exists to catch."
    }
  ],
  "notes": {
    "peer_attributable": true,
    "oracle_check": "encoding/ecf_key_ordering",
    "oracle_note": "provenance. It cites ENTITY-CBOR-ENCODING §4.2 (Content Hash Computation); the ordering rule is §4.1 Rule 2. Whether it measures emitted-frame order or hashed order is not known from the citation (F26)."
  }
}
